INT. GRAIN ELEVATOR - TOP FLOOR - NIGHT

Dust. Moonlight through slatted boards. A rusted conveyor hums, empty.

BEATRIX ALLCOTT, 30s, flashlight in her teeth, crawls along a catwalk. REUBEN STOLTZFUS, 50s, follows, clutching a pitchfork like a prayer. PAULINA ARRIAGA, 20s, brings up the rear with a camera. Her hands shake.

BEATRIX
(to the group)
Stay close. Nobody splits up.

REUBEN
Wasn't planning on it.

PAULINA
(muttering to herself)
This is a bad idea. This is a very bad idea.

A SOUND below. Wet. Dragging. They freeze. Beatrix swings the flashlight down into the dark hopper, where something pale uncoils from the corn, Reuben jabs the pitchfork toward it, Paulina stumbles back and the camera flashes white, and the thing lunges for her ankle before Beatrix hauls her up by the collar and all three scramble toward the ladder.

REUBEN
(to Beatrix)
You said it was empty!

BEATRIX
I said I THOUGHT.

PAULINA
(to no one)
It's slower. It's slower now.

They reach the ladder. Reuben climbs first. Beatrix pushes Paulina after him, then turns to look back.

Nothing in the hopper. Just a ripple in the grain, settling.

BEATRIX
(quietly)
It's gone.

REUBEN (O.S.)
Beatrix. Come on.

She climbs. Stops halfway. Looks down once more. The grain is perfectly flat.

BEATRIX
(whispering)
Goodnight.
